{
  "rules": [
    "Mild upward bias overall: forecasts tend to sit ~0.2–1.0 €/t above realized levels, especially in the mid-horizon (≈days 10–25). Apply a small downward level shift to the whole path when recent prices are range-bound.",
    "Overestimation after local peaks/spikes: when the last 5–10 days show a sharp run-up, the model extrapolates too much and stays too high; realized prices mean-revert sooner. Damp post-spike slopes (reduce projected gains) and pull forecasts back toward the recent 10–20d average.",
    "Insufficient drawdown/too-smooth during sell-offs: around downturns the model often remains elevated and does not drop fast enough. Increase responsiveness to negative momentum (steepen early-horizon declines when last 3–7d returns are negative).",
    "Horizon-specific pattern: early horizon (≈days 1–7) is relatively well calibrated; errors grow with horizon, with the model too high in the middle and sometimes missing late-horizon rebounds. Use a horizon-dependent bias: small/no adjustment days 1–7, larger downward adjustment days 8–25, and reduce the downward adjustment again toward days 26–30 when recent momentum is positive.",
    "Turning-point lag: the model tends to recognize reversals late (keeps prior trend). Add a mean-reversion term that increases when price deviates materially from the 20–30d mean (e.g., >1–1.5σ), pulling forecasts back faster."
  ]
}
